BareProxy load test: the design note's steady-load test, 5 October 2026

The design note (section 12) says: "Twenty applies under steady load (2,000 requests per second over HTTP/1.1,
HTTP/2 and WebSocket) cause no failed requests and no reset connections." live/load-test.sh is that test: it builds
bareproxy, testapi and loadtest from the tree, starts three test backends and BareProxy, sends 2,000 requests per second
for 60 seconds (800 over HTTP/1.1 on 16 keep-alive connections, 800 over HTTP/2 on 4 connections, 400 WebSocket messages
per second on 20 connections, half of them ws:// and half wss://) and makes 20 `bareproxy apply --yes` during the run, one every
2.5 seconds from the 4th second. After each apply it asks both sites for /hello, /api/x and /moved/x and checks that the new
config is already in force. Pass means 0 failed requests, 0 reset connections, 0 lost WebSocket messages and 20 applies done.

The machine is a 2-CPU cloud VM (Intel Xeon at 2.1 GHz under KVM) shared with other workers. Their builds and tests (mostly
'bp.test', the go test run of the BareProxy package) were using the CPUs during most of these runs, so the latencies below
come from a busy machine and differ between runs for that reason. 'Busy' is the share of both CPUs in use over the 60 seconds,
from /proc/stat, and it counts everything on the machine. 'Others' is the CPU time of the busiest processes that aren't part of
the test and were still alive when the run ended: builds and test binaries that finished during the run aren't in it.

Runs, in order. All 9 passed.

